import Native

{-
   The bulk operations over any container - Implementation-Vector.md §9 items 6 and 7,
   Design-Vector §4.3.

   Apart from `Container.yana`, which declares what a container *is*: nothing here is part of that
   interface, and a container author writes none of it. Seven operations, each one declaration and
   one replacement, and the same shape seven times over - which is what makes it worth reading on
   its own and worth not reading at all while looking at the classes.

   This is where "idiomatic code is fast code" is delivered. `sum(xs)` over a container of a lane
   type is a vector loop; over a container of anything else it is the loop it always was; and the
   program that wrote it said neither. Every one of them is over `Chunked`, so every container in the
   language has them, and none of them mentions a vector in its signature.

   **Each is a baseline and an `@replaces` over it** - Design-Replacements.md. The baseline is the
   element walk, and it is the whole of what the operation *means*: it is resolved on every build,
   and `-no-replacements` runs every program on it. The replacement is the vector walk, and its
   condition is the one constraint its signature adds - `Num(Vec(a))`, `Lanewise(Vec(a), Mask(a))`
   or `Bitwise(Mask(a))`. Where the element has a vector on this target that constraint holds and the
   vector body is chosen; where it does not - `[String]`, or `[Long]` on JavaScript, where `Vec(a)`
   cannot be formed at all - the constraint fails, which is not an error but the answer, and the
   baseline runs. That is the question the compiler used to answer with a role per operation and a
   copy of the rule in each resolve stage; a constraint states it once.

   The vector bodies are written over `vectors` and `vectorsMasked` rather than over `loadVector`,
   which is what makes them four lines each: the protocol already walks the chunks, reads the tail
   under a mask and folds to one loop for a contiguous container. Which of the two an operation takes
   is decided by its identity - `+` and the counting ones take `vectors`, because the lanes past the
   end contribute zero; `*`, `min` and `max` take `vectorsMasked` and force their own, because zero
   is not the identity of any of them.

   `maximum` and `minimum` take the answer for an empty container as an argument rather than
   answering a `Maybe`. It is what a fold over a container of borrows can promise without copying an
   element out of one, and it is what the vector body wants anyway: the seed is what the accumulator
   is splatted from and what the masked-off lanes hold.
-}
pub fn (Chunked(c, a), Num(a)) sum(xs: c) -> a:
    let &acc = 0 :: a

    for v in items(xs):
        acc = acc + v

    return acc

@replaces
fn (Chunked(c, a), Num(a), Num(Vec(a))) sum(xs: c) -> a:
    let &acc = zero() :: Vec(a)

    for v in vectors(xs):
        acc = acc + v

    return horizontalSum(acc)

pub fn (Chunked(c, a), Num(a)) product(xs: c) -> a:
    let &acc = 1 :: a

    for v in items(xs):
        acc = acc * v

    return acc

@replaces
fn (Chunked(c, a), Num(a), Num(Vec(a))) product(xs: c) -> a:
    let ones = splat(1 :: a)
    let &acc = ones

    for {v, live} in vectorsMasked(xs):
        acc = acc * select(maskUpTo(live) :: Mask(a), v, ones)

    return horizontalProduct(acc)

pub fn (Chunked(c, a), Ord(a), TrivialCopy(a)) maximum(xs: c, ifEmpty: a) -> a:
    let &acc = ifEmpty

    for v in items(xs):
        if v > acc then acc = v

    return acc

@replaces
fn (Chunked(c, a), Ord(a), TrivialCopy(a), Lanewise(Vec(a), Mask(a))) maximum(xs: c, ifEmpty: a) -> a:
    let &acc = splat(ifEmpty)

    for {v, live} in vectorsMasked(xs):
        acc = max(acc, select(maskUpTo(live) :: Mask(a), v, acc))

    return horizontalMax(acc)

pub fn (Chunked(c, a), Ord(a), TrivialCopy(a)) minimum(xs: c, ifEmpty: a) -> a:
    let &acc = ifEmpty

    for v in items(xs):
        if v < acc then acc = v

    return acc

@replaces
fn (Chunked(c, a), Ord(a), TrivialCopy(a), Lanewise(Vec(a), Mask(a))) minimum(xs: c, ifEmpty: a) -> a:
    let &acc = splat(ifEmpty)

    for {v, live} in vectorsMasked(xs):
        acc = min(acc, select(maskUpTo(live) :: Mask(a), v, acc))

    return horizontalMin(acc)

{-
   Counting, where the mask is the answer rather than something applied to one: `count` on a mask is
   how many lanes hold, and the lanes past the end of a chunk hold nothing because `vectors` has
   already selected the identity into them - and the identity of a comparison against `wanted` is
   whatever `wanted` is not. That is not something this can rely on, so the masked form is what it
   takes and the live mask is what it counts against.
-}
pub fn (Chunked(c, a), Eq(a)) occurrences(xs: c, wanted: a) -> Int:
    let &total = 0

    for v in items(xs):
        if v == wanted then total = total + 1

    return total

@replaces
fn (Chunked(c, a), Eq(a), Bitwise(Mask(a))) occurrences(xs: c, wanted: a) -> Int:
    let sought = splat(wanted)
    let &total = 0

    for {v, live} in vectorsMasked(xs):
        total = total + count(and(v .== sought, maskUpTo(live) :: Mask(a)))

    return total

{-
   The search, and the one operation here that leaves its loop early.

   `firstSet` is what makes the vector body a search rather than a scan: it answers the lowest set
   lane of a mask in three instructions and with no branch, so the iteration that found the element
   is also the one that says where in it - and the running count of live lanes is the position of the
   vector, which is exactly what `vectorsMasked` hands over beside it.

   **`at` is a `Size` and that is worth an instruction per iteration.** Written as an `Int` it is the
   same sequence as the iterator's own index over a contiguous container - both start at zero and
   both step by the width - and nothing could see that, because they were different types: the loop
   carried two counters and a second `add`, and every answer went through a `movslq` on the way out.
   At the same type they are one register. Measured on `test/bench/text`'s single-unit scan, which is
   this function under `String.findAscii`.

   **The baseline is the one scalar walk in this file that is still an index loop.** Every other one
   became `for v in items(xs)`, which is what the iteration protocol is for. This one answers *where*
   rather than *what*, so the position is half the result - and `items` yields elements and not
   positions. A counter beside the loop would work and would be the same two variables this already
   has, spelled twice. `at` is what the vector body returns as well, so the two answer the same thing
   in the same units.
-}
pub fn (Chunked(c, a), Eq(a)) indexOf(xs: c, wanted: a) -> Maybe(Size):
    let &at = 0 :: Size

    for chunk in chunks(xs):
        let n = length(chunk)
        for i in 0 .. n:
            if chunk[i] == wanted then return Just(at + i)

        at = at + n

    return Nothing

@replaces
fn (Chunked(c, a), Eq(a), Bitwise(Mask(a))) indexOf(xs: c, wanted: a) -> Maybe(Size):
    let sought = splat(wanted)
    let &at = 0 :: Size

    for {v, live} in vectorsMasked(xs):
        let hits = and(v .== sought, maskUpTo(live) :: Mask(a))
        if any(hits) then return Just(at + (firstSet(hits) :: Size))
        at = at + (live :: Size)

    return Nothing

-- Reached through `indexOf`, so it is one function rather than an eighth pair. A search that stops
-- at the first hit is what both bodies of that one already are.
pub fn (Chunked(c, a), Eq(a)) contains(xs: c, wanted: a) -> Bool = indexOf(xs, wanted) is Just(_)

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Comparing two containers element by element
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The seventh operation, and the one that takes two windows rather than one - which is why it is
   written over `Flat(a)` where the six above are written over `Chunked(c, a)`. Two containers of the
   same type need not be chunked the same way, so there is no lockstep walk of two `chunks`
   iterators to write; a contiguous window is what both sides of a comparison have to be reduced to
   before any of this can be a vector loop at all, and `elements` is what reduces them.

   **Equal lengths are the caller's**, not this function's: the `Eq` instances below it test that
   first, and what is compared here is `left`'s length in both windows. A comparison of two windows
   of different lengths is a question this has no answer for and does not ask.
-}
pub fn (Eq(a)) itemsEqual(left: [a], right: [a]) -> Bool:
    let count = length(left)
    for at in 0 .. count:
        if left[at] != right[at] then return False

    return True

{-
   Natively this is `Native.compareMemory`, which is the vector walk with the element type taken out
   of it: whole registers under `all`, and one overreading compare for everything below a register.
   Both windows are storage the language allocated, so the tail read is Design-Vector §8's and is
   exactly what that guarantee is for. The `Ordering` it answers costs nothing here - the equal
   answer is the path with no differing lane on it. Its condition is the constraints that say the
   element is a lane - a `[U8]`, a `[I32]` or a `[Double]` is a register a turn, and a container of
   records is the loop it always was.

   **On JS there is no replacement**, and this is the one bulk operation where that is the right
   answer rather than a missing implementation. A host vector is `lanes` values (Design-Vector
   §7.2), so `loadVector` there builds a sixteen-element array per iteration and `loadVectorTail`
   does sixteen clamped reads - work every one of the operations above has to do anyway, because each
   of them visits the whole container. This one **stops at the first difference**, so reading a
   register's worth ahead of the test is strictly more work than the walk it would replace, and it
   allocates to do it. There used to be a JS declaration here whose whole body was a call of the
   element walk; the baseline is that walk, so a JS build simply has nothing to choose.
-}
@replaces @platform(native)
fn (Eq(a), Ord(a), TrivialCopy(a), Lanewise(Vec(a), Mask(a)), Bitwise(Mask(a))) itemsEqual(left: [a], right: [a]) -> Bool =
    compareMemory(left.items, right.items, length(left)) == EQ

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The set bits of a bitmap
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   How many bits are set across a container of machine words - Analysis-Avx512.md §4.8's first row,
   and the eighth of these pairs.

   **`totalBits` and not `countBits`**, which is the name the operation has on one value: that one is
   `Bits(a)`'s member, and a plain function may not share a name with a class member any more than
   with another plain function. The distinction the two names draw is worth having anyway - one of
   them answers about a value and the other about a container, and a reader seeing `countBits(xs)`
   would have to know which.

   **Over `U64` and not over every lane type**, which is the one place this family pins its element.
   A bitmap is a run of words and `U64` is what one is made of here; the general version would have
   to widen a per-element count into the accumulator, and there is no conversion that does so for
   every integer - `Widen(a, Size)` relates the narrow ones and `Truncate(a, Size)` the wide ones,
   and a constraint can only ask for one of them. Nothing wants a bit count over a `[U8]` badly
   enough to pay for two bodies.

   **The vector body counts in whatever lanes the register has and the lane width does not matter**,
   which is the whole reason the accumulator cannot overflow: the set bits of a block are the set
   bits of a block however it is divided, so the count is taken at sixty-four lanes and each lane
   adds at most sixty-four per iteration. A `Vec(U8)` accumulator would wrap after thirty-one blocks
   and is what a body written at the element's own width would have used.

   `vectors` and not `vectorsMasked`, on the same identity argument `sum` makes: the lanes past the
   end are zero, and a zero word has no bits set.

   **`@platform(native)` and not a `Bits(Vec(U64))` constraint**, which is what the condition would
   have been if the element were a variable: a constraint over types the signature does not bind is
   refused outright by `compiler/resolve_mir` - "a constraint names types this signature binds" -
   and the element here is written down. The platform is the honest condition anyway: a host vector
   is `lanes` values (Design-Vector §7.2), so the block form on JS would be the walk with an array
   allocated per iteration, which is `itemsEqual`'s argument below.
-}
pub fn (Chunked(c, U64)) totalBits(xs: c) -> Size:
    let &total = 0 :: Size

    for word in items(xs):
        total = total + (truncate(countBits(word)) :: Size)

    return total

@replaces @platform(native)
fn (Chunked(c, U64)) totalBits(xs: c) -> Size:
    let &acc = zero() :: Vec(U64)

    for v in vectors(xs):
        acc = acc + countBits(v)

    return truncate(horizontalSum(acc)) :: Size
